(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-3-oral-cavity-salivary-quiz"]={"title":"Oral Cavity and Salivary Glands Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 3","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which test is most sensitive and specific for herpes simplex?","choices":["Polymerase chain reaction for HSV DNA","Tzanck smear","Viral culture","Potassium hydroxide preparation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Diagnosis is often clinical when characteristic lesions are present, but confirmation is by polymerase chain reaction for viral DNA, which is the most sensitive and specific option. Serology with IgG and IgM helps distinguish HSV 1 from HSV 2. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is the classic triad of peritonsillar abscess?","choices":["Fever, cough and rhinorrhoea","Stridor, drooling and tripod positioning","Neck stiffness, torticollis and a widened prevertebral space","Trismus, uvular deviation and dysphonia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Trismus is the most reliable of the three, from irritation of the adjacent medial pterygoid; uvular deviation follows oedema and medial displacement of the soft palate and peritonsillar fold; and dysphonia arises from vagus nerve involvement failing to elevate the palate. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 99"},{"q":"Why is cervical lymphadenopathy described as a sign rather than a diagnosis?","choices":["It has infectious, inflammatory, degenerative and neoplastic causes","It always resolves without treatment","It occurs only in children","It is diagnostic of lymphoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lymphadenopathy is a finding that many processes produce, from toxoplasmosis, tuberculosis, brucellosis, herpes, syphilis, cytomegalovirus, HIV, histoplasmosis and chickenpox through to neoplasia. Treatment is directed at whichever underlying cause is found, with drainage if there is an abscess. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 91"},{"q":"What antibiotic regimen is used for Ludwig angina?","choices":["Azithromycin alone","Penicillin with metronidazole, or ampicillin-sulbactam","Ceftriaxone with vancomycin","Penicillin VK alone for 10 days"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The flora is mixed oral, including streptococci, staphylococci, Bacteroides, Fusobacterium and Klebsiella in patients with diabetes, so cover includes anaerobes. Clindamycin and selected cephalosporins are alternatives, alongside external drainage if the airway is threatened and a dental consultation for the offending tooth. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 111"},{"q":"Which spaces are involved in Ludwig angina?","choices":["The space between the tonsillar capsule and pharyngeal muscles","The parapharyngeal space alone","The floor of the mouth with the submental, sublingual and submandibular spaces","The retropharyngeal space"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is a severe cellulitis of those spaces, and it is an emergency because it can rapidly compromise the upper airway and force a surgical airway. The tongue is displaced upwards and backwards by posterior spread, which is the mechanism of the obstruction. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 108"},{"q":"Which finding distinguishes oral candidiasis from leukoplakia?","choices":["The patches are painless","The patches are confined to the tongue","The patches fluoresce under ultraviolet light","The patches can be rubbed off with a tongue depressor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Candida forms a removable pseudomembrane, so wiping it leaves an erythematous base. Leukoplakia is defined as a white lesion that CANNOT be scraped off, and lichen planus produces striae that are part of the mucosa. That single manoeuvre separates three diagnoses with very different implications. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 89"},{"q":"When do the signs of rheumatic fever appear after the infection?","choices":["2 to 3 weeks, sometimes as early as one or as late as five","Within 24 hours","6 to 12 months later","Only after repeated infections over years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The delay reflects the mechanism: cross-reactive antibodies produced against the streptococcus take time to build and then attack heart muscle. Peak incidence is between 5 and 15 years, rare before 4 and after 40, and the illness typically resolves after about six weeks. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 78"},{"q":"Where do vocal cord nodules form?","choices":["At the anterior commissure","At the junction of the anterior third and posterior two thirds","At the posterior commissure","On the false cords"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. That point is where the folds strike each other hardest during phonation, so it is where mechanical trauma concentrates. The lesions are smooth, paired, whitish, bilateral and symmetric, which is the finding that separates them from a unilateral polyp. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which of these is a listed cause of stomatitis?","choices":["Excessive fluoride intake","Hypercalcaemia","Chronic sinusitis","Chemotherapy or radiation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The causes listed include trauma from ill-fitting dentures or braces, cheek and tongue biting, surgery, chemotherapy and radiation, viral infection with herpes or coxsackievirus, candidiasis, xerostomia, tobacco, nutritional deficiency of zinc or iron, allergy, and idiopathic aphthous ulceration. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 13"},{"q":"What proportion of patients with Behcet syndrome have oral ulcers?","choices":["About 10 per cent","About 40 per cent","About 75 per cent","Up to 100 per cent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Oral ulcers are the commonest feature of Behcet, affecting up to 100 per cent of patients, with genital ulcers in about 75 per cent that look identical to oral aphthae. Diagnosis is clinical: recurrent aphthous ulceration in the context of the characteristic systemic manifestations. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 19"},{"q":"What does a positive IgM against Epstein-Barr virus indicate?","choices":["Chronic carriage without infection","Current infection","Past infection","Immunity from vaccination"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. IgM appears during acute infection and IgG persists afterwards, so IgM positive means current and IgG positive means past. Serology is particularly useful in patients under four years, in those with a negative heterophile test, and where symptoms are atypical. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 84"},{"q":"What distinguishes leukoedema from leukoplakia at the bedside?","choices":["Leukoedema can be scraped off with a tongue depressor","Leukoedema is painful and leukoplakia is not","Leukoedema occurs only on the tongue","Leukoedema disappears when the mucosa is stretched"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Leukoedema is a normal variant caused by fluid accumulating within epithelial cells, so stretching the mucosa disperses it and the greyish-white change vanishes. Leukoplakia is a premalignant lesion that cannot be scraped off or stretched away, which is why this single manoeuvre separates a reassurance from a biopsy. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 9"},{"q":"What proportion of unilateral solitary anterior cervical adenitis relates to beta-haemolytic streptococcus?","choices":["Under 5 per cent","About 70 per cent","About 20 per cent","About 10 per cent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Local ear, nose and throat infections drain to regional nodes, and about 70 per cent of these unilateral solitary anterior nodes relate to beta-haemolytic streptococcal infection, with 20 per cent staphylococcal including MRSA and 10 per cent viral, atypical mycobacterial or Bartonella. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 92"},{"q":"On which type of mucosa do aphthous ulcers characteristically occur?","choices":["Freely moving, non-keratinised mucosa","Keratinised, attached mucosa","The dorsum of the tongue only","The tonsillar fossae"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aphthae are found on buccal and labial mucosa, non-attached gingiva and palate, all of which are freely moving and non-keratinised. That location rule is the practical way to separate them from herpetic lesions, which favour keratinised surfaces. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 15"},{"q":"What proportion of pharyngitis is viral?","choices":["About 30 per cent","About 10 per cent","Nearly all","About 70 per cent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Roughly 70 per cent of pharyngitis is viral, caused by adenovirus, Epstein-Barr virus, herpes simplex, HIV, influenza, parainfluenza, rhinovirus, coronavirus, echovirus, enteroviruses and coxsackievirus. The remaining 30 per cent is bacterial, chiefly group A beta-haemolytic streptococcus. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 70"},{"q":"In which age group is retropharyngeal abscess commonest?","choices":["Children under 5","Adolescents and young adults","Adults over 60","Neonates"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is commonest under five, following upper respiratory infection, otitis media or sinusitis, as retropharyngeal nodes suppurate. In adults it usually follows intraoral procedures, trauma, a foreign body such as a fishbone, immunocompromise or odontogenic spread. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 103"},{"q":"What is the essential treatment for diphtheria beyond antibiotics?","choices":["Intravenous immunoglobulin","Antiviral therapy","Antitoxin, obtained from the Centers for Disease Control","High-dose corticosteroids"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antibiotics eradicate the organism but do nothing about circulating exotoxin, and it is the toxin that causes the myocarditis and neuropathy that kill. Antitoxin is given to all patients, alongside penicillin or erythromycin for 14 days and isolation until three consecutive cultures are negative. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 113"},{"q":"What is recurrent aphthous stomatitis also called?","choices":["Sjogren syndrome","Sutton disease","Behcet syndrome","Ludwig angina"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent aphthous stomatitis carries the eponym Sutton disease. The condition is self-limiting and managed with observation, with anti-inflammatories, corticosteroids, silver nitrate cauterisation and dilute rinses as options for symptom control. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which human papillomavirus subtypes cause recurrent respiratory papillomatosis?","choices":["Herpes simplex virus 1 and 2","Epstein-Barr virus","Subtypes 6 and 11, rarely 16","Subtypes 16 and 18 only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent respiratory papillomatosis is caused by low-risk human papillomavirus subtypes 6 and 11, with 16 rarely implicated. The distribution is bimodal, juvenile between 2 and 4 years and adult peaking in the 30s, and there is a 3 to 7 per cent risk of malignant transformation. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 49"},{"q":"What is the commonest cause of hoarseness?","choices":["Acute laryngitis","Vocal cord carcinoma","Vocal cord paralysis","Reflux laryngitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute laryngitis is the commonest cause of hoarseness and typically persists about a week after upper respiratory symptoms have cleared. Rhinovirus is the commonest pathogen, and management is conservative: hydration, antipyretics, voice rest, decongestants, humidification and smoking cessation. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 57"},{"q":"What are the four Ds of paediatric epiglottitis?","choices":["Drooling, diarrhoea, dehydration and drowsiness","Dysphagia, dysuria, dyspnoea and delirium","Drooling, dysphagia, dysphonia and distress","Dyspnoea, diplopia, dysarthria and dizziness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Those four summarise the paediatric presentation, alongside the classic posture of an irritable child sitting or leaning forward with the neck hyperextended and chin thrust out. Symptoms come on suddenly and progress over hours in children, more slowly in adults. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 61"},{"q":"What is first-line antibiotic treatment for streptococcal pharyngitis?","choices":["Metronidazole for 10 days","Penicillin VK for 10 days","Azithromycin for 3 days","Ciprofloxacin for 7 days"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Penicillin VK for ten days is first line, with amoxicillin as a substitute and intramuscular penicillin G where compliance or oral intake is a concern. Mild penicillin allergy allows cephalexin or cefadroxil; severe allergy needs a macrolide or clindamycin. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 77"},{"q":"What is the characteristic symptom pattern of sialolithiasis?","choices":["Recurrent swelling and pain that worsens with eating","Constant swelling with fever and pus from the duct","Painless progressive enlargement over months","Bilateral swelling with systemic viral symptoms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Eating stimulates salivary flow, and an obstructing stone traps that saliva behind it, distending the gland until pressure equalises. That is salivary colic, and it is why the history alone often makes the diagnosis before any imaging. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which complication of retropharyngeal abscess carries a 50 per cent mortality?","choices":["Cavernous sinus thrombosis","Ludwig angina","Rheumatic fever","Mediastinitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The retropharyngeal space extends from the base of the skull to the posterior mediastinum, so infection has a direct anatomical route into the chest. Mediastinitis carries a 50 per cent mortality, and other complications include respiratory distress, rupture with aspiration pneumonia, and spread into the danger space. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which imaging method is most sensitive for detecting salivary stones?","choices":["Ultrasound","Magnetic resonance imaging","Computed tomography","Plain radiography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Computed tomography is the most sensitive method for detecting stones because it resolves small calcifications anywhere in the gland or duct. Digital subtraction sialography is described separately as the most ACCURATE method, and the two claims are deliberately distinguished. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which clinical feature argues AGAINST streptococcal pharyngitis?","choices":["Cough","Tonsillar exudate","Tender anterior cervical nodes","Fever above 100.4 degrees Fahrenheit"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Absence of cough scores a Centor point precisely because its presence argues for a viral cause. Streptococcal disease gives fever above 100.4 degrees, sore throat, tender cervical nodes, dysphagia, odynophagia and abdominal pain, with exudate on the tonsils, and characteristically no cough. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 73"},{"q":"What is the first priority in managing a peritonsillar abscess?","choices":["Obtain computed tomography before any intervention","Secure the airway if needed","Start oral antibiotics and review in 48 hours","Arrange immediate tonsillectomy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Airway comes first, before any attempt at drainage. Needle aspiration and incision and drainage then treat the collection, with parenteral amoxicillin-clavulanate or clindamycin, adding MRSA cover if severe, and oral therapy for those who can tolerate it. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 101"},{"q":"What antibiotic combination is used in epiglottitis?","choices":["A macrolide alone","Metronidazole plus penicillin","A third-generation cephalosporin plus an antistaphylococcal agent","Amoxicillin alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ceftriaxone or cefotaxime with vancomycin for 7 to 10 days covers Haemophilus, pneumococcus and staphylococcus. In genuine penicillin or cephalosporin allergy the alternative is vancomycin with a quinolone or a carbapenem, adjusted once cultures return. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 67"},{"q":"Where does herpes simplex virus lie dormant between recurrences?","choices":["The oral epithelium itself","The trigeminal ganglion","The salivary glands","The regional lymph nodes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. After primary infection the virus travels along the axon to the trigeminal ganglion and persists there. Reactivation is triggered by stress, trauma, immunosuppression or ultraviolet light, and the virus migrates back along the axonal sheath, which is why a burning prodrome precedes the visible lesion by about 24 hours. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 25"},{"q":"Why is tracheostomy avoided in papillomatosis?","choices":["It prevents the use of laser therapy afterwards","It converts the disease to a malignant form","It creates another squamociliary junction the papillomas favour","It causes uncontrollable bleeding from the lesions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Papillomas have an affinity for the boundaries where ciliated respiratory epithelium meets squamous epithelium. A tracheostomy creates a new such junction, giving the disease a fresh site to seed and spreading it distally into the trachea, which makes an already difficult problem worse. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 52"}]};
